{"name":"Torched Custard","hex":"#ffe19d","fetched":"2026-10-18","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"torched","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["simple past and past participle of torch"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/torched"},{"term":"custard","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A type of sauce made from milk and eggs (and usually sugar, and sometimes vanilla or other flavourings) and thickened by heat, served hot poured over desserts, as a filling for some pies and cakes, or cold and solidified; also used as a base for some savoury dishes, such as quiches, or eaten as a stand-alone dessert; egg custard.","A similar sauce made from milk and cornflour or custard powder (and usually sugar, and sometimes vanilla or other flavourings) and thickened by heat."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/custard"}],"entries":[{"title":"Custard","blocks":[{"text":"Custard is a variety of culinary preparations based on sweetened milk, cheese, or cream cooked with egg or egg yolk to thicken it, and sometimes also flour, corn starch, or gelatin. Depending on the recipe, custard may vary in consistency from a thin pouring sauce to the thick pastry cream used to fill éclairs. The most common custards are used in custard desserts or dessert sauces and typically include sugar and vanilla; however, savory custards are also found, e.g., in quiche."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/Custard.jpg/330px-Custard.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Custard","wikidata":"Q625825"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/Custard.jpg/500px-Custard.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/Custard.jpg/1280px-Custard.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb